// The battle for Åland, 28 June 1921 (hypothetical): four days after the League of Nations gave Åland to Finland,
// Stockholm refuses the ruling and sends troops for Eckerö, on the main island's west shore facing Sweden, under the
// coastal fleet; Finland's coastal squadron comes out of the archipelago to stop the landing. History's Swedish class
// (Sverige, Drottning Victoria and Gustaf V, the last rushed out of the yard), and the Finnish class a decade early: the
// Väinämöinens that the 1920s navy debates bought in the game (Väinämöinen and Ilmarinen, and Lemminkäinen and
// Joukahainen of the game's own). The torpedo boats are invented; the destroyers are Wrangel-likes (Hugin and Munin of
// the older class among them); the transports are coastal passenger steamers taken up (13 kn).
// The aims (docs/battle/aims.md): Sweden's three of the four transports at Eckerö, lying off it two hours while the
// troops go ashore; Finland's to stop them. Set for balance by the forces (the fourth Väinämöinen), the haze and
// Finland's bold doctrine (Aggression +1: the Kommodori fights down to a third of the odds and closes): as the AI plays
// it (24 seeds, 2026-10-31) Sweden scores about 0.57, Finland 0.45; the landing is made in half the seeds,
// Finland loses about 4 of 10 ships a run, Sweden 2 of 11. Three Sverige and three Väinämöinens: 0.96 v 0.13.
//   Sweden, the stronger ship: 283 mm guns, a 200 mm belt and 22.5 kn, and the transports to cover.
//   Finland, the numbers and the knife: big guns on small, slow, thin ships, and torpedo boats in the haze.
// The weather of the day: a thick midsummer haze on the Åland Sea (6 km), a light south-westerly.
